  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that 7,2',6'-Trihydroxy-5-methoxyflavanone may interact with Carboxylesterase 2 through a series of molecular targets, primarily SNU-387. This compound is identified as a target of multiple pathways, including (1S,3R,6S,8R,12S,15R,16R)-15-[(2R)-4-[(2R)-3,3-dimethyloxiran-2-yl]butan-2-yl]-7,7,12,16-tetramethylpentacyclo[9.7.0.01,3.03,8.012,16]octadecan-6-ol, (3R)-5,8,9-trimethoxy-2,2-dimethyl-3,4-dihydropyrano[2,3-b]quinolin-3-ol, and 5-Hydroxy-6,8-Dimethoxy-2,3-Dimethylbenzo[G]Chromen-4-One. All of these targets are known to interact with Carboxylesterase 2, indicating a potential mechanism of action where 7,2',6'-Trihydroxy-5-methoxyflavanone may influence the activity of Carboxylesterase 2 through these intermediates. The evidence suggests a possible dependency on the enzymatic activity of Carboxylesterase 2, which is further supported by the shared target relationship between 7,2',6'-Trihydroxy-5-methoxyflavanone and SNU-387, a compound that is a known substrate of the enzyme. This integrated pathway highlights the role of Carboxylesterase 2 in the metabolism of multiple compounds, potentially linking 7,2',6'-Trihydroxy-5-methoxyflavanone to its effects through this enzyme.",
